# Conditional truth and the control natives.
# Origin: REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3, sections 6.1 to 6.4.
#
# The truthiness table below is the most valuable group in the corpus for
# milestone 1. It is pure evaluation semantics with no series, no clock
# and no output, and it is where REBOL differs most sharply from the
# languages a reader is likely to arrive from. Zero is true. An empty
# block is true. Only NONE and logic FALSE are false.

--- id conditionals/true-is-true
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- code
if true [1]
--- result
1

--- id conditionals/false-is-false
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- note IF with a false condition produces none, not unset.
--- code
if false [1]
--- result
none

--- id conditionals/one-is-true
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- code
if 1 [1]
--- result
1

--- id conditionals/zero-is-true
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- note The one that catches everybody. Zero is a value, so it is true.
--- code
if 0 [1]
--- result
1

--- id conditionals/empty-block-is-true
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- code
if [] [1]
--- result
1

--- id conditionals/block-holding-false-is-true
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- note The block is the condition and blocks are true. What is inside it
--- note is never looked at, because IF does not reduce its condition.
--- code
if [false] [1]
--- result
1

--- id conditionals/none-is-false
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- code
if none [1]
--- result
none

--- id conditionals/empty-string-is-true
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.4
--- requires control
--- note Not in the guide's list, but it follows from the same rule and is
--- note the other case a reader expects to be false.
--- code
if "" [1]
--- result
1

--- id conditionals/either-takes-the-true-branch
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.1
--- requires control
--- code
either true ["yes"] ["no"]
--- result
"yes"

--- id conditionals/either-takes-the-false-branch
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.1
--- requires control
--- code
either none ["yes"] ["no"]
--- result
"no"

--- id conditionals/if-result-is-a-value
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.1
--- requires control
--- note IF is an expression, so its result can be assigned. This is what
--- note makes the language uniform: there are no statements.
--- code
flag: if true ["lunch eaten"]
flag
--- result
"lunch eaten"

--- id conditionals/condition-block-held-in-a-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.1
--- requires control
--- note The branch is an ordinary block held in an ordinary word. Code
--- note and data are the same thing, and this is the proof.
--- code
notice: ["Wake up!"]
if true notice
--- result
"Wake up!"

--- id conditionals/any-returns-first-true-value
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.2
--- requires control
--- note ANY returns the value, not a logic. This is how defaulting is
--- note written in REBOL.
--- code
number: none
any [number 100]
--- result
100

--- id conditionals/any-skips-several-nones
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.2
--- requires control
--- code
num1: num2: none
num3: 80
any [num1 num2 num3]
--- result
80

--- id conditionals/any-returns-none-when-all-fail
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.2
--- requires control
--- code
any [none false]
--- result
none

--- id conditionals/all-returns-last-value
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.2
--- requires control
--- code
all [true 1 "done"]
--- result
"done"

--- id conditionals/all-returns-none-on-first-false
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.2
--- requires control series
--- note The guide's example uses a none-valued string to short-circuit
--- note APPEND. Reduced here to the behaviour being demonstrated.
--- code
a: "REBOL/"
b: none
all [string? a string? b append a b]
--- result
none

--- id conditionals/all-runs-every-expression-when-all-pass
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 6.2
--- requires control series
--- code
a: "REBOL/"
b: "Core"
all [string? a string? b append a b]
--- result
"REBOL/Core"

--- id conditionals/error-in-a-branch-propagates
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires control
--- note An error raised inside a taken branch is the result of the whole
--- note expression. Evaluation does not carry on past it.
--- code
if true [divide 1 0]
--- error math zero-divide

--- id conditionals/error-in-an-untaken-branch-does-not-fire
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires control
--- note The untaken block is never evaluated, so what is inside it cannot
--- note fail. This is the counterpart to the previous entry.
--- code
if none [divide 1 0]
--- result
none

--- id conditionals/try-catches-an-error-as-a-value
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires control
--- note Errors are values. TRY is what stops one being raised and hands
--- note it back instead, which is why the spec insists no failure escapes
--- note as a host exception.
--- code
error? try [divide 1 0]
--- result
true

--- id conditionals/try-passes-through-a-good-result
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires control
--- code
try [divide 10 2]
--- result
5
